   "subject": "MRDS-Strata as replicates",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hello list,\r<br>\r<br>I&#39;m analyzing a narwhal aerial survey for which I have 6 strata, one of w=\r<br>hich was flown twice. We used standard MRDS sampling techniques (2 isolat=\r<br>ed observers on either side of the plane) and I&#39;m analyzing the data usin=\r<br>g the MRDS analysis engine in Distance. Due to the low density of animals=\r<br>, we have only 147 groups in the analysis, after 6 days of surveys.  So, =\r<br>I am using all of the observations to calculate one detection function wh=\r<br>ich I then use to calculate abundances by stratum. To calculate the total=\r<br> abundance, I am summing the per stratum estimates.=20\r<br>\r<br>My question is this: for the one stratum that was surveyed twice, I want =\r<br>to calculate an effort weighted mean. A concern that has been brought up =\r<br>in the past is that, since I am using the same detection curve for the tw=\r<br>o estimates, when I later combine the two estimates, I am underestimating=\r<br> the variance. This concern was brought up by a statistician but as far a=\r<br>s I understand it, since I used one detection function for both estimates=\r<br>, the Effective Strip Width is the same for both analyses and the pooling=\r<br> of the data has resulted in a lower variance of the individual stratum e=\r<br>stimates. When I then calculate the mean of these estimates, and the vari=\r<br>ance, I am underestimating the uncertainty. If I was doing a CDS or MCDS =\r<br>estimate, I could click the box &quot;strata are replicates&quot; which may take ca=\r<br>re of the problem, but there is no such box in the MRDS analysis engine. =\r<br>While I understand that the encounter rate likely contributes much more t=\r<br>o the overall variance than the ESW, is there a way to know, within an MR=\r<br>DS analysis, the breakdown of how each contributes to the variance? If so=\r<br>, can anyone then suggest a method to calculate the actual variance of th=\r<br>e mean abundance for that stratum.=20\r<br>\r<br>If this is not actually a concern, can someone please explain why so that=\r<br> I can then reply to the afore-mentioned statistician who will also be re=\r<br>viewing this analysis.=20\r<br>\r<br>Many thanks,\r<br>Natalie.\r<br>\r<br>--\r<br>distance-sampling list home page:\r<br>  <a href=\"http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792476664602000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2JDrGOtqS2X7-ovWIPhvGy\">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html</a>\r<br>To unsubscribe, go to the home page and click &quot;Join or leave the list&quot;,\r<br>  or email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jisc...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a> with the following message body:\r<br>  leave distance-sampling\r<br>Before posting to the list, read the list guidelines at\r<br>  <a href=\"http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792476664602000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2YvFdZuyw2Y79QQh2OtZyN\">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/</a>\r<br>    distance-sampling.welcome\r<br>Any questions, email the list owner at\r<br>  <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance-sam...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a>\r<br>--\r<br>"
    ]
